  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 203 AM CDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nA cold front is slowly sinking south through our area overnight and \nearly this morning then stalling out across our south later today \nbefore finally moving south of the CWA this evening. A few scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms are currently just outside our northwest \nborder and should generally stay there. Instability/support for \nstorms falls off sharply the further into the CWA you get. Some \nmodels are little more bullish with precip chances later today \npassing through from SEMO into southern IL and western KY. However, \nNBM only has some weak 20% ish isolated chances with the front \nlingering today but overall the day is mainly dry. Best chances are \nover the Ozark Foothills for a couple hours in the late afternoon. \nSome of those weak 20% isolated chances linger into Tuesday with a \nshortwave passing aloft, but again the day is mainly dry. Much \ncooler and more seasonable temperatures fall into place behind the \nfront with highs in the upper 70s and lower 80s on Tuesday. \n\nDry and seasonable conditions are in place Wednesday on through the \nweekend with a slight warm up next Sunday into Monday. Sfc high \npressure sets up over the Great Lakes and east of the lakes with \nupper level ridging over the Midwest.",
